Container networking relies on NAT to allow container network packets
out to the host's network. This requires a PF firewall to perform the
translation. A simple example is included - to use it:

# cp /usr/local/etc/containers/pf.conf.sample /etc/pf.conf
...
Edit /etc/pf.conf and set v4egress_if, v6egress_if to your network interface(s)
...
# sysrc pf_enable=YES
# service pf start

The sample PF configuration includes support for port redirections. These are
implemented as redirect rules in anchors nested under cni-rdr. 5BSD runs
OpenBSD PF: translation and filter rules share the single anchor
'anchor "cni-rdr/*"' (there is no rdr-anchor or nat-anchor), and NAT is a
'match out ... nat-to' rule.

Connections from the container host itself pass through PF on lo0 like any
other traffic, so no sysctl is needed (OpenBSD PF has no net.pf.filter_local).
Do not kldload pf by hand on a running system: 5BSD denies late module
loads. Set pf_enable="YES" so pf.ko is loaded during boot, or preload it
with pf_load="YES" in /boot/loader.conf.

If upgrading from an older version, replace any rdr-anchor/nat-anchor lines
and FreeBSD "nat on ... ->" rules in /etc/pf.conf as in the sample.

For example if host port 1234 is redirected to an http service running in a
container, you could connect to it using:

# fetch -o- http://$(hostname):1234

or

# fetch -o- http://localhost:1234
